= Solution
{parent=i}

These are <inner-sphere electron transfer> pathways distinguished by where Cr(II) attacks the coordinated thiocyanate. Cr(II) is substitution-labile and can replace an aqua ligand to form a bridged Co(III)/Cr(II) precursor.

* **Remote attack at nitrogen:** form $\mathrm{Co^{III}{-}S{-}C{-}N{-}Cr^{II}}$. <Electron transfer> through the bridge gives Co(II)/Cr(III). Cleavage on the now labile Co(II) side leaves the N-bound product $\mathrm{[Cr(NCS)(OH_2)_5]^{2+}}$. This is the faster $2.0\times10^5\,\mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}$ channel.
* **Adjacent attack at sulfur:** Cr binds the same sulfur that is attached to cobalt, giving a shared-S bridge, $\mathrm{Co^{III}{-}S(CN){-}Cr^{II}}$. <Electron transfer> and Co-S cleavage leave $\mathrm{[Cr(SCN)(OH_2)_5]^{2+}}$, the $0.8\times10^5$ channel.

In both cases the product Co(II) rapidly loses its ammines to give the hexaaqua <ion>, whereas substitution-inert Cr(III) retains the bridge-end information. The two chromium linkage isomers are therefore a record of two precursor geometries, rather than evidence that one outer-sphere electron-transfer event somehow selects two arbitrary new ligands afterwards.

\Image[paper-3-q37-bridged-transfer.png]{title=Remote and adjacent bridges retain different thiocyanate linkage information}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The free end of N-bound $\mathrm{Co{-}NCS}$ is sulfur. Cr(II) binds there, giving the remote bridge $\mathrm{Co^{III}{-}N{-}C{-}S{-}Cr^{II}}$. <Electron transfer> and cleavage of the Co-N bond leave S-bound Cr(III), $\mathrm{[Cr(SCN)(OH_2)_5]^{2+}}$; labile Co(II) becomes hexaaquacobalt(II).

The much smaller <rate constant> shows that the efficiency of bridge formation/<electron> transmission is strongly sensitive to linkage orientation. Efficient donation and coupling in a Co-S-bound precursor need not be reproduced when cobalt is N-bound. Its reduction is still consistent with <inner-sphere electron transfer>; a slow rate alone is not a criterion for outer-sphere transfer.

