= Solution
{parent=i}

A <Fischer carbene complex> typically has a low-oxidation-state, relatively electron-rich middle/late transition metal with strong pi-acceptor ligands such as CO. The <carbene> is treated as a neutral two-electron donor; metal-to-carbene back-donation contributes to its bond, but its <carbon> remains electrophilic. An OR or NR$_2$ substituent stabilizes that <carbon> by heteroatom donation. Here the metal is Cr(0), $d^6$, in an 18-electron pentacarbonyl complex.

A <Schrock carbene complex> usually contains an early metal in a high <oxidation state>, often $d^0$, with electron-donating ligands rather than a shell of pi-accepting <carbonyls>. Its alkylidene has substantial dianionic <character> in ionic counting, and its <carbon> is correspondingly nucleophilic. Alkyl substituents are common; heteroatom stabilization of an electrophilic <carbon> is not the defining feature. These are bonding/electronic limiting descriptions rather than a rule based only on the drawn metal-carbon double bond.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

CO first dissociates from the 18-electron Fischer <carbene>. Pyridyl nitrogen of C then coordinates at the vacant chromium site, positioning an ortho C-D bond of its <phenyl group> beside the <carbene> unit.

The appropriate pathway is **<oxidative hydrogen migration>**: chromium assists C-D cleavage while D transfers to the electrophilic <carbene> <carbon>. The resulting cyclometalated intermediate has both an aryl-Cr bond and an alkyl-Cr bond, the latter to $\mathrm{C(D)(OEt)(o\!-\!tolyl)}$. Formally Cr(0) becomes Cr(II). <Reductive elimination> between those two <carbon> ligands forms the new aryl–$\mathrm{C(D)(OEt)(o\!-\!tolyl)}$ bond and returns chromium to low <oxidation state>.

This accounts for all three controls. Extra CO suppresses the coordinative vacancy. Biphenyl E lacks the pyridyl-N directing group and cannot organize the necessary C-H activation geometry. The ortho deuterium moves onto the former <carbene> <carbon> rather than being lost to solvent: four D remain on the original phenyl ring and the fifth becomes the benzylic C-D bond.

This is not adequately represented as electrophilic aromatic substitution followed by proton loss, which would discard the diagnostic D. The primary mechanistic study distinguishes <oxidative hydrogen migration>/<reductive elimination> from that classical alternative.<Azacycle-directed formal aromatic C-H insertion with a chromium Fischer carbene by Xing-Qi Yao and colleagues>{ref}

\Image[paper-3-q38-chromium-migration.png]{title=Directed oxidative deuterium migration followed by carbon-carbon reductive elimination}

