\Image[paper-3-q39-sidechain-assignments.png]{title=The cinnamoyloxymethyl and tertiary-alcohol side chains, with their spectral assignments}

The two substituents are **$\mathrm{-CH_2-O-C(O)-CH=CH-Ph}$**, with the <alkene> E, and **$\mathrm{-C(OH)(CH_3)_2}$**. The free bond at the left of each drawing attaches to the <cyclohexene>.

The A-D <COSY> correlation and $16.2\,\mathrm{Hz}$ coupling identify the trans cinnamate double bond. Five aromatic protons and four phenyl-carbon environments identify its monosubstituted phenyl ring. F is a two-proton OCH$_2$ signal; its <HMBC> correlation to <carbonyl> 1 joins that methylene through <oxygen> to the <ester>. The two three-proton singlets N/O, their correlations to oxygenated quaternary <carbon> 10, and the missing OH resonance give the tertiary-alcohol substituent.

= b
{parent=39}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

\Image[paper-3-q39-sidechain-assignments.png]{title=Proton letters and <carbon> numbers assigned to the two side chains}

On the ester-containing side chain, read outward from the <cyclohexene>:
$$
\mathrm{ring{-}\underset{11;\ F,F}{CH_2}{-}O{-}
\underset{1}{C}(=O){-}
\underset{9;\ D}{CH}{=}
\underset{2;\ A}{CH}{-}
\underset{3\ (ipso)}{Ph}}.
$$
A is the downfield beta-vinylic proton and D the alpha-vinylic proton next to the <ester>.

The phenyl ortho pair is **B on <carbon> 7**; the meta pair is **C on <carbon> 6**; its para proton is the remaining **C on <carbon> 5**. Thus the C envelope integrates to three protons, while B integrates to two. <Carbon> 3 is the unprotonated ipso site.

In the other substituent, **10** is the quaternary C-OH <carbon>. The methyl assignments are **N on 13** and **O on 16**, reading the very closely spaced <HSQC> cross peaks together with the positive methyl responses in <DEPT-135>. Their mutual long-range correlations and correlations to 10 support the gem-dimethyl group. The two methyl carbons are distinguishable in this chiral molecular environment; their exact stereotopic names are not determined here.

