\Image[paper-3-q39-ring-conformation.png]{title=Preferred cyclohexene half-chair with the bulky tertiary-alcohol side chain pseudoequatorial}

Use a **<cyclohexene half-chair>**, not a fully puckered saturated-cyclohexane chair. The <alkene> carbons and their directly attached bonds form the approximately planar end; the saturated part puckers. Put the $\mathrm{C(OH)(CH_3)_2}$ substituent pseudoequatorial at the opposite saturated methine to avoid a crowded pseudoaxial arrangement. Its methine proton is correspondingly pseudoaxial. This is a preferred <conformation>; the achiral spectra do not assign an absolute configuration.

= d
{parent=39}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

\Image[paper-3-q39-ring-assignments.png]{title=Cyclohexene assignments with explicit ambiguity for the overlapping allylic methylenes}

Number ring positions $r_1$ through $r_6$ around the ring, with $r_1=r_2$ the double bond. These auxiliary ring-position labels are distinct from the printed carbon-signal numbers.

* **$r_1$:** quaternary <alkene> <carbon> **4**, attached to the OCH$_2$ side chain.
* **$r_2$:** <alkene> CH **8**, proton **E**.
* **$r_3$:** allylic CH$_2$, one of <carbon> signals **14/15**; its protons are **K and one of G/H/I**.
* **$r_4$:** methine **12**, proton **L**, attached to the tertiary-alcohol <carbon> 10.
* **$r_5$:** CH$_2$ **17**, protons **J and M**.
* **$r_6$:** the other allylic CH$_2$ **15/14**, carrying the remaining **two of G/H/I**.

The <HSQC> pairs J and M with 17 and places L on 12. It also places K and one unresolved member of G/H/I on one of the closely spaced allylic carbons, with the other two G/H/I protons on the other. <COSY> connects the vinylic E to the adjacent allylic part and connects the methine L to its neighboring methylenes; <HMBC> links F to the substituted <alkene> end and the gem-dimethyl group to the methine end.

The printed overlap does not justify a unique assignment of every G/H/I letter or a forced 14-versus-15 assignment to those two ring positions. The drawing retains those alternatives explicitly. In the half-chair, L is pseudoaxial; the resolved large/small couplings are consistent with pseudoaxial/pseudoequatorial methylene partners, but exact axial letter labels within the overlapped group remain uncertain.

