= Solution
{parent=a}

A warmer <troposphere> does not imply a warmer <stratosphere>: increased <carbon dioxide> can cool the middle and upper stratosphere by enhancing infrared emission. One direct kinetic effect is slower temperature-dependent destruction in the <Chapman cycle>, tending to increase upper-stratospheric <ozone>. Two indirect chemical effects can have the opposite sign. If the entry <atmospheric cold trap> warms, it can admit more <water>; reaction with an <electronically excited oxygen atom> then supplies more of the <HOx family>, enhancing catalytic <ozone> loss. In cold polar air, changes in <water> and <temperature> can favour <polar stratospheric clouds>, whose surfaces activate halogen reservoirs and can enhance <ozone> destruction when sunlight returns. These are conditional pathways, not a universal sign for total <ozone>: cold-trap <temperature> and circulation, halogen abundance and altitude all matter. <Methane> oxidation can also supply stratospheric <water>.<Scientific Assessment of Ozone Depletion 2022 executive summary>[Executive summary: Stratospheric Temperature, water-vapour influences, and temperature-dependent polar ozone loss]{ref}

= b
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For one specified channel, the <photolysis rate coefficient> is
$$
J(z)=\int F_\lambda(z)\,\sigma_\lambda(T)\,\Phi_\lambda(T)\,d\lambda.
$$
It depends on spectral <actinic flux>, absorption <cross section>, and channel <quantum yield>. Solar zenith angle, season, overhead absorbers, clouds and <aerosol> scattering affect the photon field; branching and collisional quenching affect the chemical yield. Absorption alone does not guarantee the desired dissociation.

**Curve A is <oxygen> <photodissociation>; curve B is <ozone> <photodissociation>.** Molecular <oxygen> needs relatively short-wavelength ultraviolet photons, which are strongly screened at lower altitudes, so its rate increases steeply with height. <Ozone> can dissociate using longer wavelengths that penetrate much further, producing the much larger, less height-sensitive rate shown by B. The plotted quantity is a first-order coefficient with units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, rather than a bimolecular <rate constant>.

