{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The dominant OH-to-HO$_2$ pseudo-first-order rate is $k_5[\mathrm O_3]=90\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, corresponding to **$0.0111\,\mathrm s$**. Recycling HO$_2$ through atomic <oxygen> and NO has rate $k_6[\mathrm O]+k_8[\mathrm{NO}]=0.0218\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, corresponding to **$45.9\,\mathrm s$** for an individual HO$_2$ radical. A fixed-total two-state exchange relaxes at the sum of forward and reverse rates, giving about $0.0111\,\mathrm s$ for the partition perturbation. Its fast partition is therefore
$$
\frac{[\mathrm{HO_2}]}{[\mathrm{OH}]}
\simeq\frac{k_5[\mathrm O_3]}{k_6[\mathrm O]+k_8[\mathrm{NO}]}
=4.13\times10^3.
$$
This supports a <steady-state approximation in chemical kinetics> for rapidly recycled intermediates if the parent <concentrations> vary slowly. It does not by itself establish stationarity of the entire <HOx family>. The later source–termination estimate gives a family turnover time of about 16 hours, far longer than partition relaxation; a stationary absolute abundance additionally assumes sufficiently persistent radiation and background conditions. This is the distinction between <fast family partition and slow family balance>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $v_i$ denote the rate of reaction $i$. For <odd oxygen> defined as O plus O$_3$, reactions 5 and 6 each consume one unit, while NO$_2$ <photodissociation> in reaction 7 restores one. Thus the loss from reactions 4–8 is $L=v_5+v_6-v_7$. HO$_2$ stationarity gives $v_5=v_6+v_8+v_4$, and NO$_2$ stationarity within this restricted recycling loop gives $v_7=v_8$. Therefore
$$
L=2v_6+v_4\simeq\boxed{2k_6[\mathrm{HO_2}][\mathrm O]}.
$$
The requested expression neglects the small termination correction $v_4$ in the odd-oxygen budget, while retaining it to determine the much slower total-radical balance. This is consistent when cycling greatly exceeds termination. The net main catalytic cycle is O plus O$_3$ forming two O$_2$ <molecules>; the NO-assisted route mainly interconverts O and O$_3$ and cancels from this restricted net loss after NO$_2$ recycling.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Using an isothermal atmosphere with the stated scale height gives $p(30\,\mathrm{km})=10^5e^{-30/7}\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $[\mathrm M]=p/(k_BT)=3.46\times10^{17}\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}}$ at $288\,\mathrm K$. The <water> <atmospheric mixing ratio> then gives $[\mathrm{H_2O}]=6.92\times10^{11}\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}}$. Applying steady state to the short-lived <electronically excited oxygen atom>,
$$
[\mathrm O({}^1D)]=\frac{J_1[\mathrm O_3]}{k_2[\mathrm M]+k_3[\mathrm{H_2O}]}
=21.7\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}}.
$$
Its <HOx family> source is $S=2k_3[\mathrm O({}^1D)][\mathrm{H_2O}]\simeq6.00\times10^3\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}s^{-1}}$. Balancing this with termination gives $2k_4[\mathrm{OH}][\mathrm{HO_2}]=S$. Combining with the rapid partition, or solving both stationary radical equations including termination, gives
$$
\boxed{[\mathrm{OH}]\simeq8.53\times10^4,\qquad
[\mathrm{HO_2}]\simeq3.52\times10^8\ \mathrm{cm^{-3}}}.
$$
Consequently the main catalytic odd-oxygen loss is
$$
\boxed{L_{\rm HO_x}\simeq1.27\times10^6\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}s^{-1}}}.
$$
The <Chapman cycle> loss is $L_{\rm Ch}=2k_9[\mathrm O][\mathrm O_3]=5.40\times10^5\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}s^{-1}}$, so the given HO$_x$ cycle is **about 2.35 times larger**. Both rates count two odd-oxygen units per completed O-plus-O$_3$ destruction event. The omitted $v_4\simeq3.00\times10^3$ correction is only about $0.24\%$ of the main HO$_x$ loss. The family abundance divided by its source is about $5.87\times10^4\,\mathrm s$, explaining why its absolute stationary state is a stronger assumption than rapid OH/HO$_2$ interconversion.

= d
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

With $[\mathrm{H_2}]=5\times10^{-6}[\mathrm M]$, a reaction of an <electronically excited oxygen atom> with <hydrogen> gives one OH and, after rapid H conversion, one HO$_2$. The radical source becomes
$$
S'=\frac{2J_1[\mathrm O_3]\left(k_3[\mathrm{H_2O}]+k_{10}[\mathrm{H_2}]\right)}
{k_2[\mathrm M]+k_3[\mathrm{H_2O}]+k_{10}[\mathrm{H_2}]}
\simeq2.85\times10^4\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}s^{-1}}.
$$
This is about 4.75 times the original source. Holding <water>, <oxygen>, NO and radiation fixed keeps the fast radical partition approximately unchanged, while bimolecular termination makes total radical abundance scale as $\sqrt S$. Thus
$$
[\mathrm{HO_2}]'\simeq7.67\times10^8\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}},\qquad
\boxed{L'_{\rm HO_x}\simeq2.76\times10^6\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}s^{-1}}},
$$
**about 2.18 times the original catalytic loss**. This predicts a stronger ozone-loss tendency within the specified stationary box model, not a numerically determined percentage change in the <ozone> layer. The latter requires coupled odd-oxygen production, changing <ozone> <concentrations>, transport and the longer-term <water> budget; those have been held fixed here.

= 43
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= a
{parent=43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Peroxyacetyl nitrate> forms reversibly from a peroxyacetyl radical and NO$_2$. It stores reactive nitrogen while intact, then thermal dissociation can release it again. It can therefore transport nitrogen through colder free-tropospheric air and recycle it more rapidly in a warmer <planetary boundary layer>. Deposition or further chemistry can compete, but reversible storage is its reservoir role.

<Dinitrogen pentoxide> is likewise a reservoir because $\mathrm{NO_2+NO_3+M\rightleftharpoons N_2O_5+M}$ can return reactive species. Conversion to nitric acid makes it a sink from the active <nitrogen oxides> family, especially through heterogeneous uptake on wet <aerosol> or cloud particles, followed by deposition or washout. <Water> vapour alone and wet-particle uptake are different mechanisms; they should not be assigned the same <rate constant>. <Temperature>, particle surface area and removal are often very different between the boundary layer and the free troposphere, so a reservoir can be transported in one environment and chemically removed in another.

= b
{parent=43}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the stated total number density, multiply the number <atmospheric mixing ratio> by $[\mathrm M]$. With NO$_x$ defined as NO plus NO$_2$,
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
[\mathrm{NO_x}]&=2.46\times10^{11},&[\mathrm{NO}]&=7.38\times10^{10},\\
[\mathrm{NO_2}]&=1.722\times10^{11},&[\mathrm O_3]&=9.84\times10^{11},\\
[\mathrm{H_2O}]&=7.38\times10^{17}
\end{aligned}\quad\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-3}}}.
$$
The <water> partial <pressure> is $30/1000=0.030$ of the total <pressure>. It is a 3% number fraction, not 30 ppm.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The fast family is **NO$_3$ plus N$_2$O$_5$**. Put $x=[\mathrm{NO_3}]$, $y=[\mathrm{N_2O_5}]$, and freeze the supplied parent <concentrations> while defining
$$
S=k_2[\mathrm{NO_2}][\mathrm O_3],\quad a=k_6[\mathrm{NO_2}][\mathrm M],
\quad b=k_7,\quad c=k_8[\mathrm{H_2O}],\quad
 d=k_4[\mathrm{NO}]+k_5[\mathrm{NO_2}].
$$
The two equations are $\dot x=S-(a+d)x+by$ and $\dot y=ax-(b+c)y$. Their stationary solution is
$$
\boxed{x=\frac{S}{d+ac/(b+c)}=3.09\times10^6\,\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-3}}},
\qquad y=\frac{ax}{b+c}=7.02\times10^8\,\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-3}}.
$$
Here $a=15.67$, $b=0.0690$, $c=3.84\times10^{-7}$ and $d=1.919$ in inverse seconds; $S=5.93\times10^6\,\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-3}s^{-1}}$. Reversible storage must not be counted as permanent family loss: only the $ac/(b+c)$ term represents the association route followed by <hydrolysis>.

Closed-family interconversion has $\tau_{\rm eq}=1/(a+b)=0.0635\,\mathrm s$. Including the supplied losses, the two linear relaxation times are approximately **$0.0566\,\mathrm s$ and $133\,\mathrm s$**, from the <eigenvalues> of $\begin{pmatrix}a+d&-b\\-a&b+c\end{pmatrix}$. Fast exchange is therefore well separated from the slower total-family adjustment. However, NO is consumed by <ozone> on $1/(k_1[\mathrm O_3])\simeq59.8\,\mathrm s$, comparable to and shorter than the family relaxation. Thus the quoted stationary <concentration> is justified for a maintained background or a sufficiently slowly varying quasi-stationary environment; it is not automatically reached in an isolated parcel initialized with the supplied composition. NO auto-oxidation is negligible at this <concentration>. Additional organic losses of NO$_3$, if present, would need to be added to the model.

= c
{parent=43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At night, <nitrate radical> addition and <ozone> attack are relevant oxidations of <isoprene>; direct daytime photochemistry is absent, though small OH sources can still exist. The nitrate-containing products identify the NO$_3$-addition route. Add NO$_3$ to the terminal <carbon> next to the methyl-substituted alkene <carbon>, producing a nitrooxy substituent and an allylic radical. Resonance moves the radical to the far terminal <carbon> while shifting the double bond. <Oxygen> trapping gives a <peroxy radical> with the <carbon> skeleton
$$
\mathrm{O_2NOCH_2-C(CH_3)=CH-CH_2OO^{\bullet}}.
$$
A nitrate-forming termination branch, for example the organic-nitrate branch of reaction with NO, replaces the terminal peroxyl group by a <nitrate ester>, giving $\mathrm{O_2NOCH_2-C(CH_3)=CH-CH_2ONO_2}$. An alternative branch forms an <alkoxy radical>, such as peroxy-radical reaction with NO$_3$ producing RO$^{\bullet}$, NO$_2$ and O$_2$. Reaction of the terminal CH$_2$O$^{\bullet}$ group with O$_2$ then gives an aldehyde plus HO$_2$, producing $\mathrm{O_2NOCH_2-C(CH_3)=CH-CHO}$. These are covalent O–NO$_2$ ester groups, not C–NO$_2$ nitro groups. The delocalized radical can be trapped after conformational change, so this mechanism does not require one universal alkene geometry for both product channels. Product structures alone do not fix a unique branching ratio.

\Image[paper-3-isoprene-products.png]
{title=Two nitrate-containing product channels from an isoprene allylic radical}
{description=Original molecular drawings of the dinitrate and nitrooxy aldehyde carbon skeletons; branching yields and a unique stereochemical ratio are not inferred from the structures alone.}

