= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Activate the <oxime> hydroxyl <oxygen> with thionyl <chloride> to give a good N–O leaving group. In the <Beckmann rearrangement> branch, the C–C bond anti to that leaving group migrates onto nitrogen as the N–O bond breaks. Here the migrating <carbon> is the spiro centre. Inserting N between that centre and the former <oxime> <carbon> expands the five-membered <oxime> ring to a six-membered <nitrilium ion> ring. <Water> attacks the electrophilic nitrilium <carbon>, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization to the observed spiro <lactam>. The <lactam> <carbonyl> <oxygen> is introduced in the aqueous work-up.

The competing <Beckmann fragmentation> cleaves the same anti C–C bond instead of completing migration onto N. Formation of the C≡N bond gives an open-chain <nitrile> and a tertiary <carbocation> at the former spiro centre. Loss of a proton from the adjacent chain <carbon> then gives the exocyclic alkene attached to the retained cyclopentane. The key distinction is **N insertion and ring expansion for the <lactam>, versus C–C cleavage followed by elimination for the unsaturated <nitrile>**. Stabilization of the resulting tertiary <carbocation> makes fragmentation plausible.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Base first gives <phenoxide>. Use its para-carbanion/quinoid resonance contributor: the para <carbon> bearing the tether attacks the terminal chloromethylene <carbon> in an intramolecular substitution, ejecting <chloride>. This forms a five-membered ring at that <carbon> and produces a **para-spirocyclohexadienone**, with the <phenoxide> <oxygen> becoming a <carbonyl> <oxygen>. The first stage is a <dearomatizing spirocyclization of a phenoxide>.

On acidification, <carbonyl> protonation makes the dienone electrophilic. A tether C–C bond attached to the spiro centre migrates to the adjacent dienone <carbon>, with the accompanying pi-bond shifts; loss of a proton restores aromaticity. This <dienone-phenol rearrangement> converts the spiro five-membered ring into the fused six-membered ring while regenerating the phenolic OH. The apparent bond formation meta to OH is thus explained by **para spirocyclization followed by skeletal migration**, rather than requiring the final fused connectivity to form directly in the base step.

\Image[paper-3-spiro-dienone.png]
{title=Dearomatizing spirocyclization followed by a dienone-phenol skeletal migration}
{description=Original numbered connectivity scheme. The highlighted tether bond changes attachment from carbon 4 to carbon 3; proton transfers and bond-order changes complete rearomatization.}

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Amide base removes a proton alpha to the <nitrile>, giving a resonance-stabilized <carbon> <nucleophile>. Removal of the available aryl proton ortho to <chlorine>, together with <chloride> elimination, generates a <benzyne> between the chlorine-bearing <carbon> and its unsubstituted ortho neighbour. The side-chain <nitrile> carbanion attacks the former chlorine-bearing aryne <carbon> intramolecularly; one aryne pi bond shifts to the neighbouring aryl <carbon>. Protonation of that aryl anion by ammonia restores the aromatic ring. The newly formed C–C bond closes the **four-membered ring of the cyanobenzocyclobutane product**. Two deprotonation processes are required to generate both the <nitrile> <nucleophile> and the aryne. The <nitrile> group is retained, and the product is not formed by simple replacement of aryl <chlorine> with an external amide <nucleophile>.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Copper-assisted loss of nitrogen from the diazo group forms a <metal carbenoid> at the <carbon> bearing the ester substituent. That tethered electrophilic <carbon> adds across the nearby exocyclic alkene in an <intramolecular cyclopropanation>. Two C–C bonds form: one to the alkene ring <carbon> and one to its terminal methylene. The existing tether thereby also becomes the five-membered ketone ring in the product; the methyl ester stays attached to the former diazo <carbon>.

Cyclopropanation is <suprafacial> on the reacting alkene face. A folded tether geometry that permits the short intramolecular closure selects the approach face and accounts for the shown relationship of the angular methyl group and the new cyclopropane methylene bridge. This is a stereoselective conformational ring-closure model, not a freely rotating intermediate <carbocation>: the two new bonds are formed in the same cyclopropanation event. A detailed quantitative preference between folded conformers would require an energetic model beyond the structure alone.

= b
{parent=44}
