= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**C has the original cyclohexadiene connectivity but trans, rather than cis, adjacent methyl groups.** Draw one methyl wedge and the other dash, keeping both phenyl groups at the diene termini. One <enantiomer> is shown below; reversing both stereobonds gives its mirror image. For the symmetric achiral starting framework under achiral conditions, the two trans <enantiomers> are not distinguished by this orbital-symmetry argument.

\Image[paper-3-electrocyclic-product.png]
{title=Trans dimethyl cyclohexadiene from thermal closure of the photogenerated triene}
{description=Original product drawing. Opposite stereobonds specify the two methyl faces; the mirror image is obtained by reversing both.}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The six pi <electrons> of B reorganize in a concerted <electrocyclic reaction>: the two terminal p orbitals form the new sigma bond, and the other two pi bonds shift to give the conjugated cyclohexadiene. Thermal six-electron closure is <disrotatory>, so the termini rotate in opposite senses. Applied to the terminal configurations drawn for B, this gives the trans methyl relationship. There is no need to invoke a radical chain or a sigmatropic transfer of a methyl group.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**A to B is a photochemical six-electron electrocyclic ring opening; B to C is a thermal six-electron electrocyclic ring closure.** The broken or formed sigma bond is between the two methyl-bearing saturated carbons of the cyclohexadiene. Both transformations conserve the <carbon> skeleton. The light-induced and thermal processes differ in their orbital occupation and therefore in their stereochemical selection rule.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <Woodward-Hoffmann rules> for a $4n+2$ <electron> <electrocyclic reaction> permit **thermal disrotation and photochemical conrotation**. Here six <electrons> <mean> $n=1$. For the ground-state six-electron chain, opposite terminal rotations match the terminal phases of its highest occupied orbital as the closing sigma bond forms. A one-electron excitation changes the relevant occupancy and reverses the allowed rotational mode. Thus A opens conrotatorily under light to the specified B, while B closes disrotatorily on heating. Reversing the rotational mode changes the adjacent methyl relationship from cis to trans, exactly as in C.

