= 47
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= a
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At fixed <particle number> and <pressure>, the joint <isothermal-isobaric ensemble> weight for a <microstate> of <energy> $E$ and <volume> $V$ is $e^{-\beta(E+PV)}$. Write its <enthalpy> as $H=E+PV$. Differentiating the <isothermal-isobaric partition function> with a temperature-independent <volume> measure gives
$$
\partial_\beta Q_P=-\int_0^\infty dV\sum_s H_s(V)e^{-\beta H_s(V)}.
$$
After division by $Q_P$ this is the normalized <ensemble average>, so
$$
\boxed{\langle H\rangle_P=-\partial_\beta\ln Q_P}.
$$
The differentiation assumes that the microscopic <Hamiltonian> and the integration measure have no additional explicit <temperature> dependence. An extra temperature-dependent normalization factor must be differentiated separately.

= b
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiate the normalized average, including the <derivative> of its denominator. The <enthalpy fluctuation formula> follows from
$$
\partial_\beta\langle H\rangle_P
=-\langle H^2\rangle_P+\langle H\rangle_P^2
=-\operatorname{Var}(H).
$$
Since $d\beta/dT=-1/(k_BT^2)$,
$$
\boxed{C_P=\frac{\operatorname{Var}(H)}{k_BT^2}}.
$$
Thus a larger equilibrium spread of <enthalpy> corresponds to a larger response to heating at fixed <pressure>. The <variance> is nonnegative, so this finite-system equilibrium description has $C_P\geq0$. The relation connects a response coefficient to fluctuations of the appropriate exchanged quantity; at fixed <volume> the corresponding quantity is <internal energy>.

= c
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Away from singular regimes, assume <enthalpy> and <heat capacity at constant pressure> are extensive: $\langle H\rangle=Nh$ and $C_P=Nc_P$, with nonzero intensive $h$. The <enthalpy fluctuation formula> then gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(H)}}{|\langle H\rangle|}
=\frac{\sqrt{k_BT^2c_P}}{|h|\sqrt N}}.
$$
Absolute fluctuations grow as $\sqrt N$, while the <mean> grows as $N$. In the <thermodynamic limit>, <enthalpy> per particle therefore concentrates around its <mean>; it is relative, rather than absolute, fluctuations that vanish. This is the usual <relative fluctuations of an extensive observable> scaling. It assumes ordinary extensive <heat capacity> and a nonzero <mean> with a stated <energy> reference; a critical divergence or an artificially zero <mean> requires separate analysis.

= d
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <volume> <integral> is a <gamma function> <integral>,
$$
\int_0^\infty V^N e^{-\beta PV}\,dV=\frac{N!}{(\beta P)^{N+1}},
\qquad
\boxed{Q_P=\frac{1}{\Lambda^{3N}(\beta P)^{N+1}}}.
$$
For the prescribed bridge relation, $\beta PQ_P=(\beta P\Lambda^3)^{-N}$, so $G=Nk_BT\ln(\beta P\Lambda^3)$. Since $\Lambda\propto T^{-1/2}$, this gives the macroscopic ideal-gas result
$$
\boxed{H=G-T(\partial_TG)_P=\frac52Nk_BT,\qquad C_P=\frac52Nk_B},
\qquad C_P-C_V=Nk_B.
$$
Heating at fixed <pressure> requires both an increase in <internal energy> and expansion work; at fixed <volume> the latter contribution is absent.

The <finite-size normalization of the isothermal-isobaric ensemble> matters here. Applying part (a) literally to the unprefactored $Q_P$ instead gives $\langle H\rangle_P=(5N/2+1)k_BT$ and $C_P=(5N/2+1)k_B$. The two answers differ because $-\partial_\beta\ln(\beta PQ_P)=-\partial_\beta\ln Q_P-k_BT$. The normalized continuous-volume distribution has $\langle PV\rangle=(N+1)k_BT$; its <variance> agrees with the unprefactored result in part (b). The bridge convention subtracts an order-one contribution and recovers the usual extensive thermodynamic answer. Both agree to leading order in the <thermodynamic limit>, but their exact finite-$N$ derivatives should not be mixed.

= 48
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= a
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With wall effects neglected, **$a_1=a$**. A second <hard disc> cannot have its centre within distance $2r$ of the first centre. Its <excluded volume> in this two-dimensional setting is an excluded area $\pi(2r)^2=4a_d$, giving
$$
\boxed{a_2=a-4a_d}.
$$
This is exclusion of the second centre, rather than simply subtraction of the first disc's own area.

= b
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At sufficiently low <packing fraction>, neglect overlaps of the excluded regions around different disc centres. Sequential insertion gives the configurational <integral>
$$
\mathcal Z_N\simeq\prod_{j=0}^{N-1}(a-4ja_d).
$$
Taking logarithms and expanding to first order in density,
$$
\ln\mathcal Z_N\simeq N\ln a-\frac{4a_d}{a}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}j
=N\ln a-\frac{2a_dN(N-1)}a.
$$
For large $N$, replace $N-1$ by $N$. The expression $N\ln(a-Nb)$ has the same leading-density expansion when **$b=2a_d$**. Thus the <hard-disc excluded-area approximation> yields
$$
\boxed{Q_{\rm ld}\simeq\frac{(a-2Na_d)^N}{\Lambda^{2N}N!}}.
$$
The factor $N!$ accounts for indistinguishable particles and $\Lambda^{2N}$ for two-dimensional momentum integration. The factor one-half between pair-excluded area and $b$ avoids counting every pair twice. Equality of leading expansions does not make this free-volume resummation exact at high density.

= c
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The surface <pressure> follows by differentiating the logarithm of the <canonical partition function> with respect to container area:
$$
\Pi=k_BT(\partial_a\ln Q)_{N,T}
=\frac{Nk_BT}{a-2Na_d}
=\frac{k_BT}{a_d}\frac{\phi}{1-2\phi}.
$$
Its formal divergence is therefore **$\phi=1/2$**. This lies outside the low-density regime used to derive the expression. It is a model pole, not an actual geometrical close-packing fraction.

= d
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

In the circular <hard-disc cage approximation>, moving the central disc towards a neighbour brings contact after a centre displacement $R-2r$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A=\pi(R-2r)^2}.
$$
At close packing, **$R=2r$ and $\mathcal A=0$**. The radius concerns the accessible positions of the moving centre; subtracting only one disc radius would incorrectly leave a finite rattling area at contact. The real cage is bounded by curved exclusion arcs, so the circle is an inscribed approximation.

= e
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Insert the stipulated near-close-packing relation for $R$ into the <hard-disc cage approximation>:
$$
\mathcal A=4a_d\left(\sqrt{\frac{\phi_{\rm cp}}\phi}-1\right)^2
=4a_d\left(\sqrt{\frac{\phi_{\rm cp}a}{Na_d}}-1\right)^2.
$$
Writing $y=\sqrt{\phi_{\rm cp}a/(Na_d)}$, we have $\partial_a y=y/(2a)$ and therefore $\partial_a\ln\mathcal A=y/[a(y-1)]$. From the supplied crystalline <canonical partition function>,
$$
\boxed{\Pi_{\rm xtl}=Nk_BT\partial_a\ln\mathcal A
=\frac{k_BT}{a_d}\frac{\phi}{1-\sqrt{\phi/\phi_{\rm cp}}}}.
$$
Its close-packing scale is set by the <triangular lattice packing of discs>, with $\phi_{\rm cp}=\pi/(2\sqrt3)$ in the ideal geometry.

= f
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The crystalline expression diverges at **$\phi=\phi_{\rm cp}\simeq0.907$**, where the cage free area vanishes. This is physically different from the spurious $\phi=0.5$ pole produced by extrapolating the low-density fluid approximation. A real compression path cannot be inferred by following that fluid pole and then abruptly switching curves. At low density it follows a fluid equation of state, while at sufficiently high density an ordered arrangement can leave more accessible rattling configurations than a disordered crowded arrangement. This is <entropy-driven hard-sphere crystallization>, here in two dimensions: allowed hard-core configurations have the same interaction <energy>, so <configurational entropy> drives the phase competition. An equilibrium first-order coexistence interval would have common <pressure> and <chemical potential>, giving a constant-pressure segment as <mean> density changes. The two asymptotic expressions alone neither locate that interval nor determine the detailed two-dimensional melting sequence. They require a consistent free-energy comparison and trustworthy approximations in the transition region.

= 49
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= a
{parent=49}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the ordinary spatial symmetries here, a <symmetry operator> is a unitary operator $U$ preserving the <Hamiltonian>:
$$
\boxed{UHU^{-1}=H},\qquad [U,H]=0.
$$
It maps an <energy> eigenstate to another state with the same <energy> and preserves the quantum dynamics. More general quantum symmetries can be antiunitary; <time reversal> is an important separate case.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

An ordinary molecular <point group> consists of the identity, proper rotations $C_n$, and, when present, reflections $\sigma$, inversion $i$, and improper rotations $S_n$. Each operation leaves the nuclear geometry and chemical labels equivalent, so only identical nuclei can be interchanged. Translations are not elements of the <point group> of a finite isolated <molecule>. The <molecular symmetry operations> determine how electronic and vibrational coordinates transform.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <spin isomers of hydrogen> illustrate how <nuclear spin statistics> constrain the product of electronic, vibrational, rotational and nuclear-spin exchange symmetries. In the ground electronic and vibrational states of these homonuclear <molecules>, the first two factors are symmetric. Exchanging the nuclei reverses the internuclear direction, so a rotational state has parity $(-1)^J$.

For $^1\mathrm H_2$, the protons are spin-one-half <fermions> and the total nuclear-exchange wavefunction must be antisymmetric. The antisymmetric <spin> singlet pairs with **<even> $J$ (para-hydrogen, one <spin> state)**; the symmetric <spin> triplet pairs with **<odd> $J$ (ortho-hydrogen, three <spin> states)**. For $^2\mathrm H_2$, spin-one deuterons are <bosons>, so the total must be symmetric. Nuclear spins $I=0,2$ supply six symmetric states paired with **<even> $J$ (ortho-deuterium)**; $I=1$ supplies three antisymmetric states paired with **<odd> $J$ (para-deuterium)**. The high-temperature ortho/para statistical ratios are $3:1$ for <hydrogen> and $2:1$ for deuterium, while low-temperature equilibrium populations also depend on rotational energies and whether <spin> conversion can occur.

= b
{parent=49}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For representation spaces $V,W$, the <tensor product of group representations> acts on product basis vectors through
$$
D_{V\otimes W}(g)=D_V(g)\otimes D_W(g).
$$
Its <character> is the <trace>. Summing diagonal elements explicitly,
$$
\chi_{V\otimes W}(g)=\sum_{i,j}[D_V(g)]_{ii}[D_W(g)]_{jj}
=\boxed{\chi_V(g)\chi_W(g)}.
$$
No step assumes one-dimensional matrices, so the result applies to both degenerate and nondegenerate representations.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a real-character irreducible molecular representation with <character> $\chi$, the multiplicity of the <fully symmetric representation> in its tensor square is
$$
m_{\rm sym}=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\chi(g)^2
=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}|\chi(g)|^2
=\boxed{1},
$$
by <character orthogonality>. The identity uses the real-character assumption appropriate to the usual real <irreducible representations> intended here. For a general complex <irreducible representation>, the <trivial representation in a tensor square> has multiplicity one precisely when it is self-dual, and zero otherwise, by <Schur's lemma>. The universally valid tensor product is $V\otimes V^*$.

= c
{parent=49}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In the class order $(E,8C_3,3C_2,6S_4,6\sigma_d)$, the 15 Cartesian nuclear displacements of <methane> have <characters> $(15,0,-1,-1,3)$. Only nuclei left fixed by an operation contribute to that operation's <trace>. Subtract translations $T_2$ and rotations $T_1$ to obtain the <methane normal-mode representation>
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{\rm vib}=A_1+E+2T_2}.
$$
Its dimension is $1+2+3+3=9=3(5)-6$. There are four frequency families: a nondegenerate symmetric stretch, a doubly degenerate bend, and triply degenerate stretch and bend modes.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose mass-weighted coordinates $Q_A$, $\mathbf Q_E=(Q_{E1},Q_{E2})$, and two triplets $\mathbf Q_3,\mathbf Q_4$ for the two $T_2$ copies. About a stationary equilibrium geometry, the allowed harmonic potential is
$$
V^{(2)}=\frac12k_AQ_A^2+\frac12k_E|\mathbf Q_E|^2
+\frac12k_{33}|\mathbf Q_3|^2+k_{34}\mathbf Q_3\cdot\mathbf Q_4
+\frac12k_{44}|\mathbf Q_4|^2.
$$
The <kinetic energy> is one-half the sum of the squared conjugate momenta. Cross terms between inequivalent <irreducible representations> are forbidden, but the two equivalent $T_2$ triplets permit the displayed scalar-product term. This is <equivalent-mode harmonic coupling>. An orthogonal rotation between the two triplets diagonalizes their $2\times2$ force-constant matrix. In <molecular normal coordinates> the complete <Hamiltonian> becomes
$$
\boxed{H=E_0+\frac12(P_A^2+\omega_1^2Q_A^2)
+\frac12\sum_{j=1}^2(P_{Ej}^2+\omega_2^2Q_{Ej}^2)
+\frac12\sum_{j=1}^3(P_{3j}^2+\omega_3^2Q_{3j}^2+P_{4j}^2+\omega_4^2Q_{4j}^2)}.
$$
Symmetry enforces degeneracies within each multiplet; it does not require equal frequencies for the two distinct $T_2$ <normal modes>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a real nonmagnetic $T_1$ electronic triplet, the <symmetric square of a group representation> decomposes as
$$
\operatorname{Sym}^2T_1=A_1+E+T_2.
$$
The $A_1$ coordinate shifts all components equally and does not remove the degeneracy. The <linear Jahn-Teller selection rule> therefore permits **$E$ and $T_2$ vibrational modes** to couple and split the triplet. Both <methane> $T_2$ vibrational triplets are symmetry-eligible. A nonzero allowed coupling generally produces a symmetry-lowering instability, although symmetry alone does not determine its strength or the preferred minimum.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The relevant decomposition is unchanged:
$$
\operatorname{Sym}^2T_2=A_1+E+T_2.
$$
Thus **the same $E$ and $T_2$ vibrational symmetries are linearly Jahn-Teller active**. Their coupling constants and the resulting <energy> surface can differ from those of a $T_1$ state. The ground-geometry <methane normal-mode representation> and the symmetry-allowed harmonic invariants are also unchanged; actual force constants in the excited state need not have the same values.

= 50
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= a
{parent=50}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Addition consumes the <carbon>–<carbon> double bond of <vinyl chloride> and retains <chlorine> as a side substituent. The interior repeat of A is therefore
$$
\boxed{[-\mathrm{CH_2-CH(Cl)}-]_n},
$$
namely <polyvinyl chloride>. <Benzoyl peroxide> supplies initiating <free radicals>; head-to-tail addition gives the usual backbone connectivity. End groups and <tacticity> are not uniquely specified by this repeat formula.

\Image[paper-3-vinyl-copolymers.png]
{title=Representative backbone segments of the chloride polymer and the two copolymers}
{description=Original labelled segments. The copolymer rows illustrate one possible sequence, not regularly repeating three-to-one blocks; the feed ratio alone does not determine incorporated composition.}

= b
{parent=50}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**<Radical polymerization>, a type of <chain-growth polymerization>.** Peroxide bond cleavage initiates reactive chains; propagation adds <monomer> to a radical end while regenerating a radical end on the longer chain. Termination and chain transfer can then stop or redirect growth. In the propagation step there is no obligatory small-molecule condensate, so this is not a step-growth condensation.

= c
{parent=50}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

B contains both $-\mathrm{CH_2-CH(Cl)}-$ and $-\mathrm{CH_2-CH(OCOCH_3)}-$ monomeric units distributed along the same <carbon> backbone: **a <vinyl chloride-vinyl acetate copolymer>**. One possible local segment is
$$
\mathrm{\cdots-CH_2-CH(Cl)-CH_2-CH(OAc)-CH_2-CH(Cl)-\cdots},
\qquad \mathrm{OAc=OC(O)CH_3}.
$$
The ester is attached through <oxygen> to the backbone <carbon>. A <random copolymer> should not be drawn as two long homopolymer blocks. The stated $75:25$ <monomer> feed is not enough to prove an incorporated $75:25$ unit ratio: <monomer> reactivity ratios and conversion affect composition and sequence statistics. Under an explicitly ideal equal-incorporation assumption that ratio could be used as a schematic average, not a periodic repeat.

= d
{parent=50}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Taking the stated melting point as the reference for a crystallizable A, introducing acetate-bearing units disrupts regular sequences and crystal packing. The expected result is **a lower melting point for B**, often with broader or less complete crystallization. The ideal <copolymer melting-point depression> estimate is
$$
\frac1{T_m}-\frac1{T_m^0}\simeq-\frac{R}{\Delta h_f}\ln x_{\rm crystallizable}>0
$$
when unlike units are excluded from the crystal. The larger vinyl ester in the drawing is <vinyl pentanoate>: its acyl substituent has a four-carbon alkyl chain. At comparable incorporation it should generally disrupt regular packing more and favour a still lower melting range or suppressed crystallinity, although the exact trend depends on composition and side-group interactions. The formula concerns melting of crystallites, not the glass transition; a universal numerical melting point cannot be inferred for an unspecified real PVC copolymer.

= e
{parent=50}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

<Saponification> cleaves the acetate ester side groups while the intended <carbon> backbone remains intact:
$$
\mathrm{-CH_2-CH(OCOCH_3)-+OH^-\longrightarrow-CH_2-CH(OH)-+CH_3CO_2^-}.
$$
The ideal target C is therefore **a <vinyl chloride-vinyl alcohol copolymer>**, containing $-\mathrm{CH_2-CH(Cl)}-$ and $-\mathrm{CH_2-CH(OH)}-$ units in the original sequence positions. The <carbon>–<oxygen> bond to the backbone remains; <hydrolysis> removes the acetyl group from <oxygen>. Incomplete <hydrolysis> would leave a three-component population of <chloride>, acetate and alcohol units. New hydroxyl groups can strengthen adhesion and coating interactions through <hydrogen bonds>.

= f
{parent=50}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The difficulty is competing chemistry and isolation selectivity. Alkaline treatment can promote <dehydrohalogenation> at chlorinated backbone units while hydrolysing the acetate side groups. Loss of HCl produces unsaturated segments, potentially followed by further degradation or cross-linking; hydroxyl formation also changes solvent compatibility and intermolecular association. The isolated material can therefore differ from the ideal undegraded C in part (e). <Competing dehydrochlorination during copolymer saponification> explains why selective conditions are needed.

This is not an absolute prohibition on hydroxyl-containing <chloride> copolymers. Analytical work on these copolymers used staged alcoholysis/<saponification> and <polymer> precipitation to restrain dehydrochlorination. The sensible conclusion is that clean, exhaustive conversion and isolation are difficult under unselective conditions, rather than that no suitable treatment can ever preserve <chloride> units.<Determination of vinyl acetate in vinyl chloride-acetate copolymers by Takanori Shimazaki and others>[Abstract, pp. 619–623: staged treatment and precipitation used to restrain competing dehydrochlorination]{ref}
